# What must not be uploaded when a deploy is driven from a workstation.
#
# `scripts/post-deploy-wizard.mjs` runs `vercel --prod --archive=tgz`, which
# tarballs the working directory and ships it to Vercel as deployment source.
# The Vercel CLI does **not** read `.gitignore` — it ignores that file as a
# *rule source*, and applies a hard-coded list of 26 entries instead (read out
# of the installed CLI: `.git`, `.next`, `node_modules`, `.vercel`, `.env.local`,
# `.env.*.local`, and twenty others). So this repo's "secrets never enter the
# repo" rule, which is a `.gitignore` promise, does not reach that upload.
#
# This file is additive: the CLI applies its own list first and then these, so
# nothing here can re-enable `.next` or `node_modules`. Never add a `!` negation
# for those. Never create a `.nowignore` beside this file either — the CLI
# fails the build with CONFLICTING_IGNORE_FILES rather than merging them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials. This is the reason the file exists.
#
# `.gitignore` covers `.env*`; the CLI covers only `.env.local` and
# `.env.*.local`. Everything else this repo generates — `.env.manual` (the one
# file a human types secrets into), `.env.vercel` and `.env.github` (both
# rendered from the AWS credentials secret by `scripts/distribute-env.mjs`) —
# matched nothing and was uploaded verbatim. The wizard writes and reads all
# three *before* it calls `vercel --prod`, so a workstation that has run it once
# had them in every subsequent deploy tarball.
.env*
!.env.example

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The local database. 70 MB, and it is a *database*: whatever a developer has
# been testing with is in it. Gitignored, therefore invisible to the CLI.
.pglite

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local build and test output. All gitignored, none of it read by the build.
screenshots
test-results
playwright-report
blob-report
.reg-report
coverage
*.tsbuildinfo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prose and repository furniture. `scripts/vercel-build.mjs` runs
# check-migrations, check-migration-graph, db:migrate and `pnpm build`, and none
# of them opens any of this. Every reference to `docs/` under `src/` is a link
# inside a comment, not a read — checked before excluding.
#
# `docs/` is the big one: 472 files, 5,725,158 bytes on disk, **2,085,157 bytes
# gzipped — 8.9% of the whole 23.6 MB upload.** It is the largest thing in the
# tarball that nothing in the build can see.
docs
.claude
.github

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A build output that only *looks* like a source file. `public/manifest-sw.js`
# is written by `scripts/build-service-worker.mjs` from `src/worker/manifest-sw.ts`,
# and `pnpm build` is `pnpm build:sw && next build` — so Vercel regenerates it
# and the copy sitting in a developer's working tree is stale by definition.
# It is gitignored, which is exactly why the CLI could not see it.
public/manifest-sw.js

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT excluded, each for a reason worth knowing before you add it:
#
#   patches/          `pnpm-workspace.yaml` declares a patchedDependency there;
#                     without it `pnpm install` fails before the build starts.
#   scripts/          `vercel.json`'s buildCommand is `node scripts/vercel-build.mjs`,
#                     and `src/db/migrations.postgres.test.ts` imports from it.
#   drizzle/          `scripts/vercel-build.mjs` runs `check-migration-graph`,
#                     which shells `drizzle-kit check` — and that walk reads the
#                     per-migration `snapshot.json` files. They are 80 MB on disk
#                     and 3.3 MB of the gzipped upload, and cutting them is the
#                     obvious saving the first time somebody reads a build log.
#
#                     It is still refused, but the reason changed on 2026-09-04
#                     and this paragraph used to give the old one. It said the
#                     exclusion "does not fail" — that `prepareOutFolder` filters
#                     the list through `existsSync`, so an empty list passes
#                     **vacuously** and the guard goes quietly green. That was
#                     true, and it was the whole problem: issue #1336 fixed it.
#                     `scripts/check-migration-graph.mjs` now counts the folders
#                     and the snapshots first and refuses a tree that has
#                     migrations and not one snapshot, naming this file in the
#                     message.
#
#                     So excluding them no longer lies — it **fails the
#                     production deploy outright**, at `vercel-build.mjs`, before
#                     `db:migrate` runs. Detectable is better than silent, and it
#                     is still not the same as correct: the snapshots stay.
